---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="The World Tipiṭaka Edition — tipitaka2500.github.io" lang="en">
  <main>
    <div class="hero-banner">
      <h1>The World Tipiṭaka Edition</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        “The Buddhist Era 2500 Great International Council Pāḷi Tipiṭaka, Roman Script 2005 / 2020
        Edition” (<em>Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500</em>)
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="home-intro-layout">
      <div>
        <p>
          This website presents a complete digital reconstruction of the <strong>40-volume
          Roman-script World Tipiṭaka Edition</strong>—the 25-century-old teaching of the Buddhist
          Theravāda tradition in the original Pāḷi language from the B.E. 2500 (1957) Great
          International Buddhist Council, updated to the <strong>2020 Edition</strong> with complete
          critical apparatus footnotes, live cross-references, page concordances, and scholarly
          indices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Derived from the authoritative <strong>Chaṭṭhasaṅgīti (Sixth Buddhist Council)
          Edition</strong> convened in B.E. 2500 (1956–1957) at Yangon by 2,500 erudite Theravāda
          Buddhist monks from around the world, the Roman-script edition is the work of{' '}
          <a href="/project.html"><strong>The World Tipiṭaka Project</strong></a> (1999–2024),
          initiated by the M.L. Maniratana Bunnag Dhamma Society Fund under the patronage of His
          Holiness Somdet Phra Ñāṇasaṁvara, the 19th Supreme Patriarch of Thailand.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          To eliminate printing mistakes found in earlier manuscripts, canonical Pāḷi experts
          recited the entire 40-volume text <strong>4 times</strong> between B.E. 2543–2545
          (2000–2002) and B.E. 2549–2550 (2006–2007) while collating against 18 international
          editions across national scripts—verifying all <strong>2,708,706 words</strong>
          (<strong>9,442,442 syllables</strong> / <strong>20,606,104 Roman letters</strong>) across
          the 88 books and 20,189 sections of the Vinayapiṭaka, Suttantapiṭaka, and
          Abhidhammapiṭaka.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
          While this website preserves 100% textual fidelity to the official World Tipiṭaka Edition database,
          a version incorporating verified textual corrections and collated errata is available for research
          and study in Markdown format at{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500</strong></a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="home-volume-figure">
        <picture>
          <source type="image/webp" srcset="/images/tipitaka-volumes.webp" />
          <img
            src="/images/tipitaka-volumes.jpg"
            alt="The 40 volumes of the Roman-script World Tipiṭaka Edition (Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500) arranged in a circle"
            width="743"
            height="575"
            fetchpriority="high"
            class="home-volume-img"
          />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="home-volume-caption">
          The 40-volume <em>Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500</em> (The World Tipiṭaka Edition in
          Roman Script)
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section aria-label="Reference Indices and Guides" class="home-index-grid">
      <a href="/guide.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Quick Guide</h2>
          <p>
            How to read book and paragraph citations, use interactive badges and footnotes, compare
            printed editions, and pronounce Pāḷi.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">User &amp; Reference Guide →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/toc.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
          <p>
            Browse and search the structural hierarchy of all 88 books and 20,116 sections with
            canonical paragraph and division ranges.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">20,116 Sections →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/verses.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Index of Verses (Gāthā)</h2>
          <p>
            Search and compare all canonical verse paragraphs side-by-side in Roman Pāḷi and Thai
            script, linked directly to each passage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">28,395 Verses →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/notes.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Critical Apparatus &amp; Notes</h2>
          <p>
            Explore manuscript variants across Sīhaḷa, Syāmaraṭṭha, Kambuja, Maramma, and PTS
            editions, plus linked cross-references.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">26,529 Critical Notes →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/concordances.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Page Concordances</h2>
          <p>
            Cross-reference paragraphs and divisions with volume and page numbers across 8 major
            printed Tipiṭaka editions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">164,784 Concordances →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/words.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>Pāḷi Word Index</h2>
          <p>
            Look up any headword across the 41 Pāḷi alphabet letters with Roman script, Thai script,
            IPA transcription, and book frequencies.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">146,693 Headwords →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/project.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>The World Tipiṭaka Project</h2>
          <p>
            History and innovations (1893–2024): from the Chaṭṭhasaṅgīti Council and Roman-script
            transliteration to Sajjhāya phonetics.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">Project History (1999–2024) →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/about.html" class="home-index-card">
        <div>
          <h2>About This Website</h2>
          <p>
            Learn how the site was rebuilt from OpenPāḷi with Astro, deterministically verified, and
            updated to the 2020 edition.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="home-index-stat">Site &amp; Edition History →</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section aria-label="Research and Open Source Downloads" style="margin: 2.5rem 0 2rem;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--text-emphasis); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
        Research, Offline Viewing &amp; Open Source Downloads
      </h2>
      <div class="home-index-grid" style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="home-index-card"
        >
          <div>
            <h2>Tipiṭaka in Markdown (with Corrections) ↗</h2>
            <p>
              Looking for raw text for computational linguistics, NLP analysis, or offline study?
              We maintain a complete version of the Tipiṭaka in Markdown format with verified textual
              corrections and collated errata applied.
            </p>
          </div>
          <span class="home-index-stat">tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500 on GitHub →</span>
        </a>

        <a
          href="https://github.com/ChristineTham/pali_grammar"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="home-index-card"
        >
          <div>
            <h2>Pāḷi Grammar Dictionary (Multiple Formats) ↗</h2>
            <p>
              Download pre-compiled offline dictionary packages for <strong>Apple Dictionary</strong> (macOS),
              <strong>GoldenDict</strong>, and <strong>MDict</strong> from the releases page, or build custom
              formats from source with textbook grammar notation symbols.
            </p>
          </div>
          <span class="home-index-stat">ChristineTham/pali_grammar on GitHub →</span>
        </a>

        <a
          href="https://github.com/tipitaka2500/tipitaka2500.github.io/releases"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="home-index-card"
        >
          <div>
            <h2>Offline Edition &amp; PWA ↗</h2>
            <p>
              Read the entire 40-volume Tipiṭaka offline anywhere! Install this site as a <strong>Progressive Web App (PWA)</strong>
              on mobile and desktop, or download the self-contained <strong>offline zip archive</strong> (~354 MB).
            </p>
          </div>
          <span class="home-index-stat">GitHub Releases &amp; Offline Zip →</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p>
      This website is created and maintained by <strong>Chris Tham</strong> (<a href="mailto:chris@christham.net">chris@christham.net</a>), author of the online <a href="https://christham.net/buddhavacana/pali-textbook/0-status/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pāḷi Textbook</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <img src="/images/cc-zero.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="16" height="16" class="cc-icon" />
      CC0: This work has been marked as dedicated to the public domain.{' '}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">See the CC0 Deed</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
